 "confidence": "medium",
 "notes_for_editor": "Reports from before the 1890s were not in the local corpus, so the 1846-1853 story rests on Putnam (1917) and LighthouseFriends (facts only, own words). The USCG Historian's page (reportedly automated 1938) could not be read (403) and was dropped by the fact-checker. Sources 5 and 9 page numbers are scan pages. The Miami Herald (2021) reportedly says the lighthouse was sold to a nonprofit for $83,000, but the page timed out, so the current owner is stated as unconfirmed. Joshua Appleby's death in the 1846 hurricane is asserted by some sources but not stated in the story. Putnam says the first tower fell burying the inmates; LighthouseFriends says no trace remained. USLHS roster ends 1933; Coast Guard-era crews not covered. Roster lines match the archive's printed list; John R. Flaherty's 'Joseph Ximenez' as first intended keeper omitted. Sand Key Ridge entries in the Commissioner reports are buoys, not this station. Note Tower 3 (2014) in the record is the nearby structure showing the light.",
